
{"id":"54f97f6e-c18d-4e75-8529-60cba7533780","object":"chat.completion","created":1785001752,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) based on the problem description. The task is SE-CR-02, which appears to be about \"chunk reduction schedule\". The description says: \"Apply deterministic chunk-removal and granularity-refinement passes while preserving a supplied syntax and interestingness oracle.\" But the actual implementation must be derived from the given fixtured payloads. The payload contains \"items\" (list of strings) and \"required_failure_items\" (list of strings). There's also \"_case_nonce\" but likely irrelevant. The output must be an object with \"attempts\", \"one_minimal\", and \"reduced_items\".\n\nWe need to implement a reduction algorithm. Based on the description: Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity. Return reduced items, attempt count, and one_minimal check.\n\nInterpretation: We have a list of items. We want to reduce it by removing chunks (contiguous sublists) while ensuring that all required failure items are still present after removal. The algorithm: Start with granularity = 2. At each granularity, try removing chunks of that size from left to right. If removing a chunk still leaves all required failure items, accept that removal (i.e., actually remove it), and then restart with slightly coarser granularity (maybe increase granularity by 1? or double?). The description says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" And \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" This is a bit ambiguous: \"slightly coarser granularity\" might mean increase by 1, but then \"double\" when no removal succeeds. Let's parse carefully.\n\nThe text: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\"\n\nInterpretation: There is a granularity parameter g. Initially g=2. Then we iterate over the list trying to remove chunks of size g (contiguous). For each chunk position (left to right), check if removing that chunk still leaves all required failure items. If yes, permanently remove that chunk, and then set g = g + 1 (slightly coarser) and restart the entire process from the beginning of the new list? Or continue? \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" suggests that after a successful removal, we increase g (maybe by 1) and start over from the leftmost of the current list. Then if we go through all possible chunk positions at current g and no removal succeeds, then we double g (g = g*2) and continue? But it says \"stop at singleton granularity\" - that means when g becomes 1? Actually \"singleton granularity\" indicates granularity of 1? But we start at 2, and we might increase until it reaches the length of the list? Or we stop when g is 1? That seems odd because we start at 2 and increase, never decreasing. \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean we stop when the granularity equals the size of the list (i.e., one chunk covering the whole list) or when it's 1? The word \"singleton\" usually means size 1. But the algorithm described: start at 2, after a successful removal increase granularity slightly (maybe by 1), and when no removal succeeds double granularity. So granularity can become larger, possibly exceeding the list length. When that happens, we stop? Or we stop at \"singleton granularity\" meaning when the chunk size equals 1? That doesn't align with starting at 2. Let's think: Maybe the granularity refers to the number of pieces to split the list into? Or the chunk size? The phrase \"granularity two\" could mean chunk size 2. Then \"slightly coarser granularity\" could mean chunk size 3. \"Double granularity\" could mean chunk size 4,8,... So we increase chunk size. When chunk size becomes 1? That would be finer, not coarser. So \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean stop when chunk size equals the length of the list (i.e., one chunk covering everything)? That would be \"singleton\" as \"one piece\". Typically \"singleton\" means one. So perhaps granularity refers to the number of chunks? Or more likely it's chunk size and we stop at chunk size = len(items) (i.e., trying to remove the entire list) but we check if required failure items remain; if the entire list is removed, then no failure items remain, so it won't be accepted. So the algorithm would terminate when chunk size > len(items). Or maybe stop at chunk size = 1 after doubling? That doesn't make sense.\n\nLet's re-read: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\"\n\nAlternative: Granularity might mean the number of chunks into which the list is divided. For example, granularity 2 means split into 2 chunks, and try removing each chunk. Then granularity 3 means split into 3 chunks, etc. \"Slightly coarser granularity\" would mean more chunks (finer granularity? Actually coarser granularity means fewer, larger chunks. So granularity 2 is coarser than 3? Typically granularity refers to size of pieces: finer granularity means smaller pieces. So \"granularity two\" might mean two chunks? That would be coarse. \"Slightly coarser\" would mean even fewer chunks, like 1 chunk? That doesn't match doubling. Doubling the number of chunks makes finer granularity. So this interpretation is inconsistent.\n\nGiven the outputs include \"one_minimal\" (boolean) likely indicating whether the reduced result is minimal with respect to single-item removals? Possibly the algorithm checks if removing any single item (granularity 1) still preserves required failure items. That would be \"one_minimal\". The algorithm description says \"stop at singleton granularity\", which could mean we also try granularity 1 (singleton). But the process starts at 2, then after successful removal we increase granularity slightly (maybe 3), then no success leads to doubling (4,8,...), but eventually when granularity becomes too large, we might then go to 1? Or perhaps the order: starting at chunk size 2, iterate through positions, if a removal succeeds, increase chunk size by 1 and restart. If no removal succeeds, double chunk size. When chunk size becomes greater than the current list length, we stop? And then we also need to check minimality at size 1? The \"one_minimal\" output suggests that after the reduction, we check if each individual item can be removed while still keeping all required failure items. If none can be removed (i.e., the list is minimal with respect to single-item removal), then one_minimal = True, else False.\n\nFrom the provided fixtures, we can deduce expected behavior. Let's look at the first case:\nitems = [\"t0\",\"t1\",\"t2\",\"t3\",\"t4\",\"t5\",\"t6\",\"t7\",\"t8\",\"t9\"]\nrequired_failure_items = [\"t0\",\"t9\"]\nWe need to run the algorithm and produce reduced_items, attempts, one_minimal. There are 3 cases, all with the same items but different required_failure_items. We can try to simulate to see what results might be. But we don't have expected outputs. We need to implement a deterministic algorithm that matches the description.\n\nLet me try to implement a plausible algorithm based on the description and common reduction strategies (like delta debugging). Delta debugging is a classic algorithm for minimizing failing test cases. It works by trying to remove subsets (chunks) and checking if the failure still occurs. Here \"required_failure_items remain\" is the condition: after removal, the set of required failure items must be a subset of the remaining items. So we want to reduce the items list while ensuring that all required failure items are still present.\n\nThe algorithm described resembles a version of delta debugging but with specific granularity progression. In standard delta debugging, you start with granularity 2 (split into 2 subsets), try each subset (chunk) removal, if a removal succeeds, you continue with that reduced set and granularity 2 again. If no removal succeeds, you increase granularity (split into more subsets) and try again. This is similar to the description: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" However, standard delta debugging doubles the number of subsets (granularity) when no removal succeeds, and after a removal, it resets to granularity 2. \"Slightly coarser granularity\" might mean after a removal, you increase the chunk size? In delta debugging, after a successful reduction, you go back to granularity 2 (finest). Here it says \"slightly coarser\", which is confusing. Could be a misphrasing.\n\nGiven the task name \"chunk_reduction_schedule\", perhaps it's a custom algorithm. Let's design a deterministic algorithm:\n\n- Let items be a list of strings.\n- required = set(required_failure_items)\n- Define a function check(items): return required.issubset(set(items))\n- We want to repeatedly remove chunks (contiguous segments) while the check passes.\n- Start with chunk_size = 2.\n- While chunk_size >= 2 and chunk_size <= len(items):\n    - For each start index from 0 to len(items)-chunk_size (inclusive):\n        - candidate = items[:start] + items[start+chunk_size:]\n        - if check(candidate):\n            - items = candidate\n            - chunk_size = chunk_size + 1? (slightly coarser)\n            - break out of for loop and continue while loop (restart from left)\n    - If no removal succeeded (for loop completes without break):\n        - chunk_size = chunk_size * 2\n- After the while loop ends (when chunk_size > len(items) or chunk_size < 2?), we also need to attempt singleton removal for the one_minimal check? The output requires one_minimal boolean.\n\nBut the description says \"stop at singleton granularity\". Maybe after the main loop, we try chunk_size = 1? Or maybe the process includes chunk_size 1 as part of the main loop? Starting at 2, then after no success we double to 4,8,... eventually when chunk_size > len(items), we stop. But where does singleton come in? Possibly after the while loop, we do a final phase with chunk_size = 1 (singleton) to see if any single item can be removed while preserving required items. That would determine one_minimal. If no single item removal is possible, then one_minimal = True. Else False.\n\nAlso, \"attempts\" is likely the number of checks (i.e., number of times we evaluate the check function). That includes both successful and failed attempts.\n\nWe also need to consider that the algorithm might restart after an accepted removal with \"slightly coarser granularity\". If we take \"slightly coarser\" to mean increment by 1, then after a successful removal, chunk_size increases by 1. That could lead to slower progression. But then \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\" means when we fail, we double the chunk size. This would make chunk size increase rapidly if no success for a while. This seems plausible.\n\nBut we must ensure that the algorithm terminates. Since chunk_size increases, eventually it will exceed len(items) and we stop.\n\nOne more nuance: The chunks are tried from left to right. If a removal is accepted, we \"restart\" with the new list and the new granularity (increased by 1). That means we go back to the start of the new list and try chunk_size again.\n\nNow, we need to implement this in Python.\n\nEdge cases: What if the required failure items are not present initially? The check would fail initially? But the inputs likely have them present. Also, after removal, we must keep them.\n\nWe need to count attempts: each call to check is an attempt. Even if we break out early after a successful check, that's one attempt. We should count them.\n\nNow, let's write solve(case) accordingly.\n\nBut we also need to consider the \"one_minimal\" check: After reduction, we check each item individually: for each i, if removing items[i] still leaves all required items, then one_minimal = False. Otherwise True.\n\nLet's attempt to simulate for the first case to see if it produces reasonable results.\n\nCase 0: items = t0..t9, required = {t0,t9}. Suppose we start chunk_size=2, try removing [t0,t1]: new list missing t0 -> fails because t0 missing. Next chunk [t1,t2]: remove t1,t2 -> still has t0,t9? yes. So accept. Remove t1,t2. New items: [t0,t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9]. chunk_size becomes 3. Now try 3-chunk removal from start: [t0,t3,t4] -> remove t0 -> fails. Next [t3,t4,t5] -> remove those, remaining [t0,t6,t7,t8,t9] -> still has t0,t9 -> accept. New items: [t0,t6,t7,t8,t9]. chunk_size=4. Try 4-chunk removal: [t0,t6,t7,t8] -> remove t0 -> fails. Next [t6,t7,t8,t9] -> remove t6,t7,t8,t9 -> loses t9 -> fails. End of list, no success. Double chunk_size to 8. Now len=5, chunk_size=8 > len, so loop ends? Actually we stop when chunk_size>len. So final items = [t0,t6,t7,t8,t9]? That seems plausible. Then one_minimal check: try remove t0 -> fails (loses t0). try remove t6 -> succeeds (still has t0,t9) so one_minimal=False. attempts count: number of checks: first removal (chunk2) 2 checks (fails then success). Second removal (chunk3) 2 checks (fail then success). Then no removal for chunk4 (2 checks, fail fail). Total 6 checks. Then chunk8 0 checks because >len. So attempts=6. Reduced_items = [\"t0\",\"t6\",\"t7\",\"t8\",\"t9\"]. That seems like a valid reduction.\n\nBut we need to confirm if this matches the expected behavior. The description \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" might mean after a removal, we increase granularity by 1 (as we did). \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" we did. \"stop at singleton granularity\" - we never tried granularity 1. So maybe we should also try granularity 1 after the main loop? That would be a separate phase to check minimality, not part of reduction. But the phrase \"stop at singleton granularity\" might indicate the algorithm includes granularity 1 as the final step. Perhaps the loop continues until granularity becomes 1 (i.e., after doubling, it might become 1? No, doubling from 2 gives 4, not 1. So maybe the algorithm decreases granularity? Unlikely.\n\nAnother interpretation: \"granularity\" might refer to the number of pieces to split into, not chunk size. Start at 2 pieces. Try removing each piece. If a removal succeeds, increase number of pieces (finer granularity) by 1? That would be \"slightly coarser\" because more pieces means finer? Actually coarser means fewer pieces, finer means more pieces. So \"slightly coarser\" would be fewer pieces, i.e., decreasing the number of pieces. But the algorithm says \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" – doubling the number of pieces makes finer granularity. This is confusing.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll stick with the interpretation that chunk size = granularity, start at 2, increment after success, double after failure, and stop when chunk size > list length. Additionally, after the main loop, we attempt singleton removal for the one_minimal check only. I think that is plausible.\n\nLet's also consider the \"one_minimal\" check definition: \"one_minimal\" likely indicates whether the reduced list is minimal in the sense that no single item can be removed while preserving the required items. So after reduction, we test each item.\n\nNow, let's code it with careful counting of attempts.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) that takes a payload dict.\n\nWe must ensure deterministic, no randomness.\n\nNow, produce the JSON with implementation.\n\nOne more thought: The description says \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: syntax_valid, interestingness_preserved, granularity_progression, no_stale_cache.\" But these are likely part of the overall system, not directly relevant to our implementation. We just implement the algorithm.\n\nLet's write the Python function. We'll define inner function check(items_list) that returns bool. We'll use a loop.\n\nPseudo:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case[\"items\"][:]  # copy\n    required = set(case[\"required_failure_items\"])\n    attempts = 0\n    chunk_size = 2\n    while chunk_size <= len(items):\n        removed_any = False\n        for start in range(0, len(items) - chunk_size + 1):\n            candidate = items[:start] + items[start+chunk_size:]\n            attempts += 1\n            if required.issubset(candidate):\n                items = candidate\n                chunk_size += 1  # slightly coarser\n                removed_any = True\n                break\n        if not removed_any:\n            chunk_size *= 2\n    # one_minimal check\n    one_minimal = True\n    for i in range(len(items)):\n        candidate = items[:i] + items[i+1:]\n        attempts += 1\n        if required.issubset(candidate):\n            one_minimal = False\n            break\n    return {\n        \"attempts\": attempts,\n        \"one_minimal\": one_minimal,\n        \"reduced_items\": items\n    }\n\nBut note: In the main loop, after we accept a removal, we increase chunk_size by 1. However, we also break out of the inner loop and continue the while loop with the new list and new chunk_size. That matches \"restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\".\n\nWe must ensure we don't skip positions because after removal, the list length changes, and we start from left again.\n\nEdge: The algorithm as described may never attempt chunk_size=1. But we added a separate check for one_minimal. That seems fine.\n\nNow, we need to verify if the attempts count includes the one_minimal checks. Yes, we increment for each.\n\nLet's test with the first case mentally: As computed earlier, attempts = 2 (first chunk2) + 2 (second chunk3) + 2 (chunk4) = 6, plus one_minimal checks: we removed t6? Actually after reduction items = [t0,t6,t7,t8,t9]. Check remove t0: fail; remove t6: success -> one_minimal=False, attempts=7. So total attempts=7. That seems reasonable.\n\nBut we don't know expected outputs; we just need to produce a function that matches the described algorithm.\n\nPotential issues: The algorithm as written might have an infinite loop if chunk_size never exceeds len(items). But since we increase chunk_size after failure (doubling), it will eventually exceed. Also, after success we increase by 1, which could be <= len(items) for a while.\n\nNow, we must also consider that the algorithm may try chunk_size > len(items) after doubling; then while condition fails and we exit.\n\nOne more nuance: The description says \"stop at singleton granularity\". Perhaps after the while loop, we should also attempt chunk_size=1 as part of the reduction, not just for minimality check. But then the one_minimal check becomes redundant because it would be the same as checking if removal of size 1 succeeds. However, the algorithm says \"stop at singleton granularity\", implying that the reduction process includes trying chunks of size 1. So perhaps the main loop includes granularity 1 as a final step. In that case, we need to incorporate chunk_size=1 into the algorithm. How? Possibly after doubling and failing multiple times, eventually chunk_size becomes 1? But we start at 2, and after doubles it becomes 4,8,... never 1. Unless we have a mechanism to decrease granularity? What if after doubling, we then try granularity 1? Could be that when granularity becomes larger than list length, we then set granularity to 1 and try again? The phrase \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean the algorithm continues until it reaches granularity 1, i.e., the coarsest possible (chunk of size 1 is the finest, not coarser). Actually \"singleton\" means one, so granularity 1 means each chunk is one item. That is the finest granularity. So the algorithm starts at 2 (chunks of 2), then after success increase by 1 (3,4,...), after failure double (4,8,...). Eventually, if we keep failing at higher granularities, we might eventually try granularity=1? Only if we have a step that decreases granularity. Not in our current logic.\n\nMaybe the intended algorithm is: start with chunk size = 2 (i.e., split into 2 chunks). Try removing each chunk. If a removal succeeds, reduce to that subset and restart with chunk size = 2. If no removal succeeds, split each chunk further (increase number of chunks, i.e., finer granularity) by doubling the number of chunks. And when you reach the point where each chunk is a single item (singleton granularity), you stop. That is exactly the delta debugging algorithm. In delta debugging, the granularity parameter n is the number of chunks. You start with n=2. You try each of the n chunks (removing that chunk). If any chunk removal passes the test, you reduce to that subset and restart with n=2. If none pass, you increase n to 2*n (double the chunks) and try again. You stop when n exceeds the length of the current set (i.e., each chunk is a single element). This matches the terms: \"singleton granularity\" meaning each chunk is a singleton (size 1). The phrase \"start chunk removal at granularity two\" could mean start with 2 chunks. \"Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain.\" \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" – but after reduction, you restart with n=2, which is coarser (fewer chunks) than the current n? Actually if you just reduced, the current n might be higher, but you reset to n=2, which is coarser. That fits \"slightly coarser granularity\" (resetting to 2). \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" So double the number of chunks (finer), and stop when chunks become singletons (i.e., n >= len(items)). This is the standard delta debugging algorithm.\n\nGiven this interpretation, the algorithm changes significantly. Let's adopt this because it's a well-known algorithm (delta debugging) and the terms align.\n\nSo we need to implement delta debugging for minimization with chunk removal (complement sets). In delta debugging, we partition the list into n contiguous chunks. We try each chunk's complement (i.e., remove that chunk). If any complement passes the test, we set the list to that complement and restart with n=2. Otherwise, if none pass, we double n. We stop when n > len(items) (i.e., each chunk is of size 1 or less). This yields a 1-minimal result.\n\nThus, the output \"one_minimal\" indicates whether the final reduced list is 1-minimal (i.e., removing any single element fails). In delta debugging, the result is 1-minimal by construction because we stop when n exceeds length, meaning we have tried all single-item removals (since n=len would partition into singletons, and we try removing each singleton). Actually delta debugging with complement sets ensures that the final set is 1-minimal because when n > len, the algorithm would have attempted to remove each singleton during the last iteration (n = len). But the algorithm as described stops when n > len, so it may not have tried singleton removal? Actually in standard delta debugging, when n = len, each chunk is a singleton. You try to remove each singleton individually. If none succeed, you increase n to 2*len, which is > len, and then you stop. So the last iteration with n=len tests all singletons. So the resulting set is guaranteed 1-minimal. So one_minimal should always be True after a successful delta debugging run? Not necessarily if the initial set is not 1-minimal. But the algorithm ensures that if a singleton removal is possible, it would have been found when n=len. So the result is 1-minimal. Therefore one_minimal should always be True in the result after full delta debugging. However, the output schema includes one_minimal as a boolean, so we must compute it anyway. But if we implement delta debugging correctly, it will always be True. However, the algorithm described might be a variant that doesn't guarantee 1-minimal, or we might need to check separately after reduction.\n\nGiven the output requirement, we should compute one_minimal explicitly after reduction by checking each single item removal.\n\nLet's implement delta debugging (complement approach). We'll count attempts as the number of test calls.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\ndef delta_debug(items, required):\n    attempts = 0\n    n = 2\n    while n <= len(items):\n        chunk_size = (len(items) + n - 1) // n  # ceiling division to get chunk sizes\n        # iterate over chunks from left to right\n        start = 0\n        removed_any = False\n        for i in range(n):\n            end = min(start + chunk_size, len(items))\n            if start >= len(items):\n                break\n            chunk = items[start:end]\n            # complement = items without this chunk\n            complement = items[:start] + items[end:]\n            attempts += 1\n            if required.issubset(complement):\n                items = complement\n                n = 2  # restart at coarser granularity\n                removed_any = True\n                break\n            start = end\n        if not removed_any:\n            n *= 2\n    # after loop, items should be 1-minimal\n    one_minimal = True\n    # check each single item removal\n    for i in range(len(items)):\n        candidate = items[:i] + items[i+1:]\n        attempts += 1\n        if required.issubset(candidate):\n            one_minimal = False\n            break\n    return items, attempts, one_minimal\n\nBut note: In delta debugging, when n exceeds len(items), we stop. So singletons are tested when n == len(items) (if len is power of 2? Actually n grows by doubling, so it may skip over len. But the algorithm tries n = len exactly if len is a power of 2. Otherwise, it may go from n < len to n > len. To ensure singleton removal is tried, we need to handle the case when n becomes > len, we should have tried n = len as the last feasible n? The standard delta debugging often includes a step: if n > len, set n = len. But the description says \"stop at singleton granularity\", which likely means when n >= len(items), we stop because each chunk would be a singleton. So we can modify: continue while n <= len(items). When n becomes > len, we exit. That means we might not have tested singleton removals if len(items) is not a power of 2. To fix, we can set n = len(items) after n exceeds len? Or we can include an additional pass with n = len(items) if not already done. But the description \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity\" suggests that when we double and get n > len, we stop. That means we might not have tried all singletons if len is not a power of 2. For example, len=5, n goes 2,4,8 >5 stop. We tried n=2 and n=4. n=4 partitions into chunks of size 2,2,1,1? Actually with ceil division, chunks might be uneven. The algorithm with n=4 on 5 items: chunk_size = ceil(5/4)=2, so chunks: [0:2],[2:4],[4:5] (three chunks because last chunk shorter). That's not all singletons. So we never try n=5 (singletons). So the result may not be 1-minimal. Then we need a separate one_minimal check. That matches the output requirement. So our algorithm wi